### Where Decap CMS is better

- **Languages.** Decap CMS supports content in several languages, with three file structures. Mica has no language setting, only two documented patterns.
- **Git hosts.** Decap CMS works with GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, Gitea, Forgejo and Azure. Mica works with any Git host over HTTPS, but only a few are tested: GitHub, GitLab-style and Forgejo hosts.
- **Preview for every editor.** Decap CMS shows a preview pane while editors write. Mica has a live preview in the local editor and in the self-hosted Mica. The self-hosted Mica needs a preview container of the website.
- **Editorial workflow.** Decap CMS can open one branch and one pull request for each entry. Mica has no such step.
- **Community and age.** Decap CMS has a large community and many years of use in production. It had releases in September and October 2026.
- **A static app.** Decap CMS is a static app under /admin. With GitLab or Gitea PKCE it needs no server of your own. Decap Turbo is a hosted service. Mica has no hosted offering yet. A Mica Cloud is planned.

### Where Mica is better

- **Agents.** Mica has an MCP server, built into the free editor. It runs locally (stdio and HTTP) and in the self-hosted Mica, where each person signs in and allows each agent. An agent reads the content model, and Mica checks every value it sends. Every save carries the revision the agent read, so an agent never overwrites a newer save. Agents save drafts and publish only when allowed. Decap CMS has an experimental MCP server and editor bridge in @decap/cli, and they work only with the paid Decap Turbo.
- **Many large files.** Many large photos and files make a Git repository heavy and slow to clone. Mica keeps them in Git LFS, so the history stays small, in the local and the self-hosted editor. Decap CMS does not support Git LFS on GitHub (issue #1206 is open) or on Gitea. GitLab media works since version 3.14.0.
- **External data.** Your entries can link to data that lives elsewhere: product data, place names, regions or codes. Mica uses thousands of items from a JSON or CSV file of your website, and never edits them. The Decap CMS documentation does not describe this.
- **Git submodule.** Decap CMS does not support a content folder in a Git submodule (issue #4056, open since 2020). Mica works in the content repository and never commits in the website repository.
- **Many entries.** Without Decap Turbo, Decap CMS makes one API request for each file, against the rate limit of your Git host. Its documentation says that a thousand entries are a thousand requests. Mica reads a Git clone, so there are no host API limits. It is tested with 1,000 entries in a collection and 5,000 files.
- **Sign-in.** Decap CMS has no direct OpenID Connect sign-in. Mica self-hosted works with any OpenID Connect provider.
- **Astro.** Mica has an official Astro package. Decap CMS has a guide in the Astro documentation.
- **One commit for several changes.** The Decap CMS documentation describes no batch publish. In Mica, Publish makes one commit of the changes the editor chooses.
- **Map with zoom.** The Decap CMS map widget stores a GeoJSON string without a zoom. Mica stores the zoom with the place, so the page can show the same view.

### Equal, or not documented

- Both are MIT licensed and need no database in the free product.
- Roles: neither has them in the free product.
- Conflicts between two editors: the Decap CMS documentation does not say.
